## 1. Shortest JavaScript Program

The shortest JS program is just an empty file.\
Even then, when the browser runs that file, the JavaScript engine still
does work behind the scenes.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 2. Global Execution Context & Window Object

The global execution context creates a special object called **window**
(in browsers).

-   This object holds lots of built-in functions and variables available
    globally.
-   We can access these anywhere in the program.
-   It also creates the `this` keyword.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 3. Global Execution Context

When any JavaScript runs (even an empty file), the engine creates a
**Global Execution Context (GEC)**.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 4. `this` at Global Level

At the global level in a browser:

-   `this` refers to the `window` object\
-   So `this === window` is **true**

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 5. Undefined vs Not Defined

### Undefined

-   Undefined is the placeholder which is placed in memory.
-   A variable is declared but no value is assigned yet.

### Not Defined

-   The variable was never declared at all.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 6. JavaScript as a Loosely Typed Language

JavaScript is known as a loosely typed (or weakly typed) language.

-   Variables are not bound to a specific data type.
-   Types are determined automatically at runtime.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 7. Scope, Scope Chain & Lexical Environment

### Scope

Scope is dependent on the lexical environment.\
Scope is the area in a program where a variable can be accessed.

### Scope Chain

Scope chain is the mechanism JavaScript uses to search for variables in
nested scopes.

### Lexical Environment

Lexical environment is the local memory along with the lexical
environment of its parent.
